$DIR/main.p:16:3: cannot pass &v to (*Holder).set, which keeps it after this iteration: the loop at line 14 reuses v in every iteration
    &v is a pointer to v. v lives in f's frame, where every iteration of the
    loop at line 14 reuses the same memory for it. (*Holder).set stores it in
    h.p (main.p:9). When the next iteration starts, what (*Holder).set kept
    would see that iteration's v, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    If (*Holder).set needs the view only during the call, change it so it does
    not keep it. To use the value later, keep the value instead of a view of it:
        var saved int // declared before the loop
        ...
        saved = v
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)
